    "assessment": "The Kephalaia is a heavily composite text with rich and well-preserved cosmological substrate (~55%) wrapped in a pervasive editorial Q&A skeleton. The substrate is the impersonal correspondential teaching: numbered cosmic enumerations, mappings of realm-to-realm and being-to-being (Five Sons, Twelve Zodiacs, Seven Heavens, Ten Firmaments), zoomorphic faces of cosmic kings (lion-face, eagle-face, fish-face), and the Manichaean cosmic dramatis personae (Father of Greatness, Living Spirit, Third Messenger/Ambassador, Virgin of Light, Adamas, Atlas/Omophorus, the King of Glory, the Splendid One, the Youth, the Watchers, the Archons, the Dragon). This substrate displays the diagnostic 'both sides cosmic' quality — never reaching outside the cosmic system. It is enclosed by an editorial dialogue frame ('Again said the Illuminator to his disciples...') and supplemented by Manichaean ecclesial material (Elect/Catechumens/Presbyters, Sundays, fasting, prayer, almsgiving), polemical material against Magousaeans/Jews/Nazoraeans, and direct hortatory address ('my beloved ones'). The strongest single signal of substrate is the prevalence of ordinal-enumeration cosmic mappings (Tϣⲁⲣⲡ ⲡⲉ X / Tⲙⲁϩⲥⲛⲉⲩ ⲡⲉ Y) combined with cosmic figure names. The strongest editorial signal is the bridge phrase 'Tⲧⲁⲗⲓⲛ ⲁⲛ ⲡⲁⲭⲉ ⲡⲫⲱⲥⲧⲏⲣ.' The text uses the Lycopolitan/Subakhmimic dialect (ⲍ for ϩ in many positions). The most diagnostic substrate term is ⲡⲡⲣⲉⲥⲃⲉⲩⲧⲏⲥ (Third Messenger/Ambassador) — distinct from the editorial ⲡⲁⲡⲟⲥⲧⲟⲗⲟⲥ (Mani) — which signals cosmic context whenever it appears. The numbered cosmic enumerations (especially of the Five Worlds, Five Sons, Twelve Zodiacs, Five Star-pathmakers, Seven Powers, Three Garments) are the unmistakable substrate fingerprint."
